# Sizes: scale_size

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_size()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_size.html), which covers
`scale_radius()`, `scale_size_binned()`, `scale_size_area()` and `scale_size_binned_area()` too,
in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

The `size` aesthetic is mostly for points and text. `scale_size()` maps a continuous column to their
area, since area is what readers perceive, while `scale_radius()` maps it to their radius.
`scale_size_area()` makes a value of 0 an area of 0. `scale_size_binned()` and
`scale_size_binned_area()` are the binned counterparts of `scale_size()` and `scale_size_area()`,
cutting the column into bins first. A line's width, once also set by `size`, is now the `linewidth`
aesthetic. Giggly has none of this yet: every point it draws looks as ggplot2's default point does,
and it maps no `size` and has no scale functions.

## Size from a column

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, size = hwy)) +
   geom_point()
p
p + scale_size("Highway mpg")
p + scale_size(range = c(0, 10))
```

A scale's first argument titles its legend, and `range` sets the smallest and the largest sizes.

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `size`, `scale_size()`, whose first argument titles the legend, and its
`range` option, and the legend that reads it back.

## Zero as no size

```r
# If you want zero value to have zero size, use scale_size_area:
p + scale_size_area()
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `size`, `scale_size_area()` and the legend that reads it back.

## Binned sizes

```r
# Binning can sometimes make it easier to match the scaled data to the legend
p + scale_size_binned()
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `size`, `scale_size_binned()` and the legend that reads it back.

## Sizes from counts

```r
# This is most useful when size is a count
ggplot(mpg, aes(class, cyl)) +
  geom_count() +
  scale_size_area()
```

`geom_count()` draws one point for each place that has rows, sized by how many it has.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_count()`, `scale_size_area()` and the legend that reads the counts back.

## Size as radius

```r
# If you want to map size to radius (usually bad idea), use scale_radius
p + scale_radius()
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `size`, `scale_radius()` and the legend that reads it back.
